{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>It shall be eaten as unleavened cakes in a holy place; in the court of the Tent of Meeting they shall eat it.</b> Rashi explained: 'in a holy place' — and which is it? 'in the court of the Tent of Meeting.' Yet in any case 'in a holy place' is superfluous, and likewise one of the two — 'it shall be eaten' and 'they shall eat it' — is superfluous, and the implication of the language is that there are two aspects here: that of the eater and that of the eaten. The aspect of the eater — this means that the priest who eats it must know that his eating is from the table on high, for by rights the meal-offering should be entirely for the Most High, except that the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him his portion from His fire. If so, the priest must eat his portion in the place where the portion of the Most High is eaten, that is, in the court of the Tent of Meeting, for there is the place of the altar. And even though that place is indeed holy, in any case the requirement upon the eater is not from the side of the sanctity of the place but only from the side that he must adjoin his eating to the eating of the altar, to teach that just as the altar's eating effects atonement, so the priests' eating — through them the owners attain atonement." "But the aspect of what is eaten is the matzah: by virtue of its being free of leaven, there is an aspect of sanctity in matzah. Therefore it says, 'unleavened cakes shall be eaten in a holy place,' for on account of the aspect of the matzah there is a requirement that the place be holy. Afterward he gave a reason for both: for concerning what he wrote, 'in the court of the Tent of Meeting they shall eat it,' this is because 'their portion have I given it from My fire,' and therefore both must be in a single domain. And concerning what he wrote, 'unleavened cakes shall be eaten in a holy place,' he gave a reason and said, 'it is most holy, like the sin-offering and like the guilt-offering.' For the sin-offering and the guilt-offering, which come for atonement of sin, are called most holy, for the completely righteous man who never sinned at all is 'holy to YHWH,' but one who sinned and repented is 'holy of holies,' for in the place where penitents stand, completely righteous men cannot stand (Berakhot 34b), and our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Yoma 86b) that one who repents out of love — his deliberate sins become for him like merits, as will be explained with the help of YHWH below in the portion of Vayeleh. And this is a great degree that does not exist in the completely righteous man, besides the other virtues that our Sages, of blessed memory, mentioned there. Therefore it is just that the sin-offering be most holy. And likewise the matzah requires a holy place for this reason, for it too is most holy by virtue of the removal of the leaven in the dough, which profanes the sanctity of man." "And the reason for the removal of leaven may be explained in two ways. The first way is, according to the words of Rabbi Alexandri, who said (Berakhot 17a): 'Our will is to do Your will, but the leaven in the dough holds us back.' And along this path we explained above the verse 'all leaven and all honey you shall not burn as incense' in the portion of Vayikra — see there. And concerning this it says here, 'most holy, like the sin-offering and like the guilt-offering.' For through the slaughtering of the sacrifice, a person slaughters his evil inclination, and it is sought and behold it is gone. Therefore the meal-offering as well comes free of leaven. However, the two loaves of Shavuot were specifically leavened, for were it not for the evil inclination, those below would not need the Torah any more than those above — for with this argument Moses vanquished the angels and said: 'Is there perhaps an evil inclination among you…' (Shabbat 88b–89a). And furthermore, the existence of the evil inclination is necessary, for were it not for it, a man would not build a house nor marry a woman. But in the domain of Torah there is no concern that it may breach its boundary, for the Torah is a spice against it." "But the thanksgiving-offering had both leavened loaves and matzah alike, for the matzah as a sign that the hardship of his heart had already been subdued, as it is written concerning four who must give thanks, 'and He subdued their heart with hardship' (Psalms 107:12) — for the wicked are in control of their heart… and our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded the verse 'one who slaughters a thanksgiving-offering honors Me' (ibid. 50:23) as referring to one who slaughters his inclination and confesses over it (Sanhedrin 43b) — and from there is a proof that the slaughtering of the thanksgiving-offering is the slaughtering of the evil inclination. Therefore he brings as a sign unleavened cakes, free of leaven. But in any case he still needs the evil inclination for physical sustenance, therefore there were also leavened loaves. And there were three matzah loaves corresponding to one leavened loaf, in order to nullify its existence by its majority — for every person should divide his years into thirds in Torah: a third in Scripture, a third in Mishnah, and a third in Talmud, and a fourth part he gives to the sustenance of his body." "However, since we do not find any leaven in a sinner's meal-offering, we may say the reverse of this: that one who brings a sacrifice for his sin of his own accord, without compulsion — no trace of the inner leaven whatsoever remains in him. But one who slaughters a thanksgiving-offering, after suffering has come upon him that compels him to repentance — surely a trace of the inner leaven still remains in him. And know and understand: חָמֵץ (leaven) and מַצָּה (matzah) have the same letters, and they are alike in pronunciation, for the ה and the ח interchange in the אחעה letters; but חָמֵץ has a count of three more than מַצָּה. Corresponding to this, for every leavened loaf there were three of matzah, so that they would be equal in quantity. And there is in this a hint that the evil inclination has an advantage over the good inclination — a count of three; they are three arguments that are mentioned in the book Hovot ha-Levavot that the evil inclination has over the good inclination. And if it comes to defeat it, then the threefold cord will stand against it — in Scripture, Mishnah, and Talmud — and then the outcome between them will be: half to YHWH and half to you." "The second way is the more correct and plausible one: that matzah is a sign of self-abasement, like dough of matzah that does not rise upward. And the matzah of the festival of Pesach proves it, for it is a sign of freedom and redemption, because HaShem desired Israel only on account of their diminishing themselves, as they learned (Ḥullin 89a) from the verse ״לֹא מֵרֻבְּכֶם מִכָּל הָעַמִּים חָשַׁק ה׳ בָּכֶם כִּי אַתֶּם הַמְעַט״ (Deuteronomy 7:7). For what does HaShem ask of you but to fear (Deuteronomy 10:12) — and humility brings one to fear — and as long as self-abasement is found among the lower ones, to that degree the kingdom of Heaven is more revealed and manifest, for He, blessed be He, has clothed Himself in grandeur and there is none besides Him. And this is the reason for the Egyptian exile: to humble them in the iron furnace, and ״חָסוּ בְשֵׁם ה׳״ (Zephaniah 3:12), and through self-abasement they went out to freedom from the kingdoms and entered the yoke of the kingdom of Heaven. Therefore the law concerning leaven is specifically burning, for it is the way of praise that whoever exalts himself is judged by fire, as is found in the Midrash on the verse ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה הִוא הָעֹלָה עַל מוֹקְדָה״. For one who wishes to rise upward like the nature of leaven, which rises upward, is fit to be judged by fire, whose nature is to rise upward. Therefore the law concerning leaven is even בְּמַשֶּׁהוּ, for in all character traits a person must walk in the middle path, except for pride, concerning which our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Sotah 5a): not a little of it and not a small part of it — see that they forbade even a מַשֶּׁהוּ of pride; so too a מַשֶּׁהוּ of leaven is forbidden. And thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Avot 4:4): מְאֹד מְאֹד הֱוֵי שְׁפַל רוּחַ בִּפְנֵי כָל אָדָם, and this is an allusion to the extreme degree of the matter, to distance it from everything." "And according to the words of Rabbi Alexandri, who likened the evil impulse to the leaven in the dough, we may say that therefore they forbade even the מַשֶּׁהוּ, because our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Bereshit Rabbah 22:6): at first the evil impulse is like a spider's thread — that is, a מַשֶּׁהוּ — and afterward it swells and rises until it becomes like the thick ropes of a wagon and rises to the violence of man without remedy. Therefore a person should not belittle even a מַשֶּׁהוּ of it in his own eyes, for a little leaven swells and rises, as stated. And for this reason they would bring upon a thanksgiving offering thirty cakes of matzot, for regarding the four who must give thanks it is stated ״וַיַּכְנַע בֶּעָמָל לִבָּם״, and after self-abasement it is fitting that they offer thirty cakes of matzot. And perhaps it corresponds to the kingship that is acquired through thirty degrees, and these encompass all the kinds of exaltation belonging to a king. And this person who exalted himself — in all of them or in some of them — after his heart was humbled through suffering, he brings thirty matzot that indicate self-abasement, to say that not one remained in them, and even a מַשֶּׁהוּ is not in them. And the ten of leaven, which indicates exaltation and self-elevation — to give all the greatness and exaltation to the First Cause, blessed be He, who is comprehended in ten sefirot of withholding; and ״וּכְבוֹד אֱלֹהִים הַסְתֵּר דָּבָר״. And for this reason matzah is a sign of redemption and freedom in every place: whether in the exodus from Egypt, whether in the thanksgiving offering, as it is written (Psalms 107:2) ״יֹאמְרוּ גְּאוּלֵי ה׳ אֲשֶׁר גְּאָלָם מִיַּד צָר״ and so on — and this refers to all four who must give thanks, who were redeemed because ״וַיַּכְנַע בֶּעָמָל לִבָּם״ and so on, and therefore he brings cakes of matzot; whether in the meal offerings, for through the offerings a person is redeemed from the hand of the evil impulse, whose net is spread over him to ensnare him in the cords of sin, and he goes out to freedom from suffering and from the kingdoms and from the angel of death through the offering that brings him to a broken spirit and self-abasement. Therefore the law of meal offerings is to be baked as matzah, and they resemble a sin offering and a guilt offering in the manner that has been explained."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:25.334074029Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}